    "content": "{\"title\": \"ULTRASOUND SCAN OF BREASTS\", \"technique\": \"Technique: High resolution handheld 2D ultrasound of both breasts and axilla was done using 5-18 MHz linear probe in Mach 30 Supersonic Ultrasound Machine.\", \"normal_findings\": [\"Indication for examination: Asymptomatic Screening. / Diagnostic work-up -\", \"Study is compared with previous mammogram / ultrasound dated\", \"Tissue Composition:\", \"Tissue Pattern: Both breasts show homogeneous background echotexture – fat. / homogeneous background echotexture – fibroglandular. / heterogeneous background echotexture.\", \"Glandular Component: Minimal (< 25% glandular tissue). / Mild (25 to 49% glandular tissue). / Moderate (50 to 74% glandular tissue. / Marked (≥ 75% glandular tissue).\", \"There is no mass, non-mass lesion, cyst or calcification seen.\", \"The subareolar tissues are normal.\", \"No significant retromammary findings.\", \"The axillary tails are normal.\", \"No significant axillary adenopathy.\"], \"default_impression\": \"IMPRESSION:###Assessment: Negative Ultrasound (BI-RADS CATEGORY 1).###Management Recommendation: Annual screening with mammogram at 40 years.###Assessment: Benign finding (BI-RADS CATEGORY 2).###Management Recommendation: Annual screening with mammogram at 40 years.###Assessment: Probably benign finding (BI-RADS CATEGORY 3).###Management Recommendation: Short interval followup after 6 months.###Assessment: Suspicious abnormality (BI-RADS CATEGORY 4).###Management Recommendation: Tissue Diagnosis, Biopsy should be considered.###Assessment: Highly suggestive of Malignancy (BI-RADS CATEGORY 5).###Management Recommendation: Tissue Diagnosis, Appropriate action should be taken.###Co-read by: Dr. Suganya P / Dr. Likhitha / Dr. Binsiya / Dr. Barani###x-----------------------------------------Your report ends here --------------------------------------------x###Assessment Categories of Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System (BI-RADS):###Assessment###Management###Likelihood of Cancer###Category 0: Incomplete - Need additional imaging evaluation / Need prior Imaging for comparison###Recall for additional imaging / Need comparison to prior examinations###N/A###Category 1: Negative###Routine screening###Essentially 0% likelihood of malignancy###Category 2: Benign###Routine screening###Essentially 0% likelihood of malignancy###Category 3: Probably Benign###Short-interval (0 to 6-month) follow-up or continued surveillance (12-month)###>0% but ≤ 2% likelihood of malignancy###Category 4: Suspicious###Category 4A: Low###suspicion for malignancy###Category 4B: Moderate###suspicion for malignancy###Category 4C: High###suspicion for malignancy###Tissue Diagnosis###> 2% but < 95% likelihood of malignancy###> 2% to ≤ 10% likelihood of malignancy###> 10% to ≤ 50% likelihood of malignancy###> 50% to < 95% likelihood of malignancy###Category 5: Highly Suggestive of Malignancy###Tissue diagnosis###≥ 95% likelihood of malignancy###Category 6: Known Biopsy-Proven Malignancy###Clinical follow-up with surgeon and/or oncologist, and definitive local therapy (usually surgery) when clinically appropriate###N/A\", \"raw_text\": \"ULTRASOUND SCAN OF BREASTS\\nIndication for examination: Asymptomatic Screening. / Diagnostic work-up -\\nStudy is compared with previous mammogram / ultrasound dated\\nTechnique: High resolution handheld 2D ultrasound of both breasts and axilla was done using 5-18 MHz linear probe in Mach 30 Supersonic Ultrasound Machine.\\nTissue Composition:\\nTissue Pattern: Both breasts show homogeneous background echotexture – fat. / homogeneous background echotexture – fibroglandular. / heterogeneous background echotexture.\\nGlandular Component: Minimal (< 25% glandular tissue). / Mild (25 to 49% glandular tissue). / Moderate (50 to 74% glandular tissue. / Marked (≥ 75% glandular tissue).\\nThere is no mass, non-mass lesion, cyst or calcification seen.\\nThe subareolar tissues are normal.\\nNo significant retromammary findings.\\nThe axillary tails are normal.\\nNo significant axillary adenopathy.\\nIMPRESSION:\\nAssessment: Negative Ultrasound (BI-RADS CATEGORY 1).\\nManagement Recommendation: Annual screening with mammogram at 40 years.\\nAssessment: Benign finding (BI-RADS CATEGORY 2).\\nManagement Recommendation: Annual screening with mammogram at 40 years.\\nAssessment: Probably benign finding (BI-RADS CATEGORY 3).\\nManagement Recommendation: Short interval followup after 6 months.\\nAssessment: Suspicious abnormality (BI-RADS CATEGORY 4).\\nManagement Recommendation: Tissue Diagnosis, Biopsy should be considered.\\nAssessment: Highly suggestive of Malignancy (BI-RADS CATEGORY 5).\\nManagement Recommendation: Tissue Diagnosis, Appropriate action should be taken.\\nCo-read by: Dr. Suganya P / Dr. Likhitha / Dr. Binsiya / Dr. Barani\\nx-----------------------------------------Your report ends here --------------------------------------------x\\nAssessment Categories of Breast Imaging Reporting and Data System (BI-RADS):\\nAssessment\\nManagement\\nLikelihood of Cancer\\nCategory 0: Incomplete - Need additional imaging evaluation / Need prior Imaging for comparison\\nRecall for additional imaging / Need comparison to prior examinations\\nN/A\\nCategory 1: Negative\\nRoutine screening\\nEssentially 0% likelihood of malignancy\\nCategory 2: Benign\\nRoutine screening\\nEssentially 0% likelihood of malignancy\\nCategory 3: Probably Benign\\nShort-interval (0 to 6-month) follow-up or continued surveillance (12-month)\\n>0% but ≤ 2% likelihood of malignancy\\nCategory 4: Suspicious\\nCategory 4A: Low\\nsuspicion for malignancy\\nCategory 4B: Moderate\\nsuspicion for malignancy\\nCategory 4C: High\\nsuspicion for malignancy\\nTissue Diagnosis\\n> 2% but < 95% likelihood of malignancy\\n> 2% to ≤ 10% likelihood of malignancy\\n> 10% to ≤ 50% likelihood of malignancy\\n> 50% to < 95% likelihood of malignancy\\nCategory 5: Highly Suggestive of Malignancy\\nTissue diagnosis\\n≥ 95% likelihood of malignancy\\nCategory 6: Known Biopsy-Proven Malignancy\\nClinical follow-up with surgeon and/or oncologist, and definitive local therapy (usually surgery) when clinically appropriate\\nN/A\"}"
